# Neon Postgres connection strings — see CLAUDE.md "Backend + database
# foundation" for how to create a Neon project and where to find these.
#
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values from your own Neon
# project dashboard. Never commit .env.local or any real credentials —
# .env.local is already gitignored (see .gitignore's `.env*` pattern).
#
# Phase 23 database isolation — local development points at the same Neon
# PREVIEW branch Vercel Preview deployments use; only Vercel Production
# points at the Neon PRODUCTION branch. Use your own Neon preview-branch
# connection strings here, never the production branch's.
DATABASE_URL=
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=

# The pooled connection string (DATABASE_URL) is used by the application at
# request time (src/db/index.ts). The direct/unpooled connection string
# (DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED) is used only by Drizzle Kit for migrations
# (drizzle.config.ts) — some migration tooling behaves better against a
# direct connection than through a pooler; it is never read by the running
# application itself.

# Admin authentication (Auth.js v5, Google OAuth only, JWT session
# strategy) — see CLAUDE.md "Admin foundation" for how to create the
# Google OAuth credentials and generate a secret. Server-only; never
# prefix any of these with NEXT_PUBLIC_.

# Session signing/encryption secret. Generate with `openssl rand -base64 32`,
# or node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# — never hardcode or commit a real value. (`npx auth secret` is suggested by
# Auth.js's own docs, but in this environment it resolved to an unrelated
# package printing a BETTER_AUTH_SECRET-named suggestion — see CLAUDE.md
# "Admin foundation" for the full note. Verify what it prints before trusting it.)
AUTH_SECRET=

# From a Google Cloud OAuth 2.0 Client ID (Web application type).
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=

# Media Library (Phase 15) — Vercel Blob, authenticated via Vercel's OIDC
# federation, NOT a long-lived BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN. @vercel/blob (2.x)
# auto-detects both of these from the environment; nothing in the app code
# references them by name directly.
#
# Both are populated automatically by `vercel env pull` once a Blob store
# is linked to this project (Vercel dashboard -> Storage -> your store ->
# Connect Project, or `vercel link` first if the project isn't linked yet).
# Never commit real values; never prefix either with NEXT_PUBLIC_.

# Short-lived OIDC identity token — expires and must be refreshed locally
# by re-running `vercel env pull` (or restarting `vercel dev`) periodically
# during local development. This is expected, not an error.
VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN=

# The target Blob store's stable ID (format: store_xxxxxxxxxxxx). Unlike
# VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN this does not expire — find it in the Vercel dashboard
# under Storage -> your Blob store -> Settings if `vercel env pull` doesn't
# populate it.
BLOB_STORE_ID=

# Legacy/alternative auth path — a long-lived read-write token scoped to
# one store. Not required when VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN + BLOB_STORE_ID are both
# set; @vercel/blob only falls back to this if OIDC auth isn't available.
# Leave unset under the OIDC flow.
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=

# Stripe (Phase 21C-2A onward) — environment-aware as of Phase 23. Every
# key/secret below MUST match the deployment environment's expected mode —
# src/data/deployment-environment.ts is the one place that expected mode is
# decided (from Vercel's own VERCEL_ENV variable, never inferred from
# NODE_ENV alone), and src/server/payments/stripe.ts /
# src/components/CheckoutPaymentStep.tsx refuse to initialize the payment
# provider at all if the configured key's own prefix doesn't match. See
# CLAUDE.md "Stripe PaymentIntent Creation (Phase 21C-2B)", "Stripe Payment
# UI (Phase 21C-2C)", and the Phase 23 section for the full design.
#
# Expected mode by deployment environment (VERCEL_ENV — set automatically
# by Vercel on Preview/Production; genuinely absent for local `next dev`,
# which is treated as "development" as long as NODE_ENV isn't "production"):
#
#   Local development (no VERCEL_ENV) → sk_test_ / pk_test_ / a TEST-mode webhook secret (e.g. from `stripe listen`)
#   Vercel Preview (VERCEL_ENV=preview)   → sk_test_ / pk_test_ / a Preview-specific TEST-mode webhook secret, only if a webhook endpoint is registered for Preview (Preview URLs are ephemeral — see CLAUDE.md's Phase 23 section for why a stable endpoint isn't practical there, and Stripe CLI-based testing is used instead)
#   Vercel Production (VERCEL_ENV=production) → sk_live_ / pk_live_ / the real, registered production webhook's live signing secret
#
# A key/secret whose own prefix doesn't match its environment's expected
# mode is refused outright before any network access — never silently
# accepted, never coerced into the "closer" mode.
#
# The secret key. Server-only; never prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# The publishable key. Deliberately browser-visible (NEXT_PUBLIC_-prefixed)
# — publishable keys are designed by Stripe to be exposed client-side; they
# can create tokens/confirm payments but never move money or read account
# data on their own.
NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# Phase 21C-2D — the signing secret for POST /api/stripe/webhook
# (src/app/api/stripe/webhook/route.ts). Server-only; never prefix with
# NEXT_PUBLIC_. Obtained from the Stripe Dashboard (or `stripe listen`
# during local development) once a webhook endpoint is configured for this
# app and environment — production's own endpoint is not yet configured as
# of Phase 23. See CLAUDE.md "Signed Stripe Webhooks (Phase 21C-2D)" for the
# full design and for exactly what manual setup is required before a real
# webhook can be sent. As of Phase 23, an event's own `livemode` value is
# also cross-checked against the deployment environment's expectation
# (development/preview: livemode=false only; production: livemode=true
# only) — this secret alone is not the only enforcement.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# OpenAI (Phase 20A onward) — Big Red Brain (/admin/brain) and AI Creative
# Studio (/admin/creative-studio). Server-only; never prefix with
# NEXT_PUBLIC_. No test/live-mode distinction exists for this provider the
# way Stripe has — the same real key is used across every environment, but
# real usage in Development/Preview incurs real, billed cost (see CLAUDE.md
# "Big Red Brain Foundation (Phase 20A)" for the daily/monthly cost
# guardrails already in place regardless of environment).
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Deployment environment (Phase 23) — src/data/deployment-environment.ts.
# VERCEL_ENV is set automatically by the Vercel platform on every Preview
# and Production deployment ("preview" / "production") — it is NEVER set
# manually here, and is genuinely absent for local `next dev`, which is
# exactly what allows local development to resolve to "development" without
# any configuration. Do not add a VERCEL_ENV= line to .env.local — doing so
# would not reflect reality and could cause local development to be
# misclassified.
#
# Database isolation (documented here for completeness — DATABASE_URL/
# DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED above are scoped per-environment in Vercel, not
# duplicated in this file):
#   Local development → Neon PREVIEW branch
#   Vercel Preview    → Neon PREVIEW branch
#   Vercel Production → Neon PRODUCTION branch
# Preview deployments (triggered by any branch/PR) must never be able to
# read or write real production business/payment data — this isolation, not
# any code-level guard, is what enforces that.
